{
  "image_name": "3124bef7b8048c7a6116a29e218e9f55.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several ways to measure light from a display and to form images from a microdisplay. On the left, a circular region of interest is overlaid on a square pixel grid. The “whole-pixel” approach counts only pixels fully inside the circle, producing all-or-nothing contributions, whereas the “fractional-pixel” approach assigns partial percentages to edge pixels, giving a more accurate estimate of the measured area.\n\nThe upper middle and right sketches show two optical setups for measuring display brightness or scattering under ambient light: an integrating sphere and an open box with diffusing walls. Blue arrows indicate light paths from the display and the surroundings toward a detector. Small example images suggest the display appears darker when off and brighter when on against the same ambient background.\n\nThe lower left panel illustrates depth-selective imaging with an aperture and a movable detecting plane. Depending on where the plane is placed, two points from a virtual image can both be visible or only one appears in focus. The lower right panel shows a Fourier-optics arrangement for a microdisplay, with lenses relaying light through a Fourier plane to a CCD sensor, emphasizing image formation in both object and Fourier space.",
